1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,910
Listen, practice, speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:18,710
Hi everyone. Welcome back to our podcast. We're really happy you're here today.

3
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:23,630
Yes, welcome everyone. If you're listening right now, whether you're walking, working,

4
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:27,110
studying, or just relaxing, thanks for spending some time with us.

5
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:33,590
Today we're going to have a very relaxed and natural conversation. No complicated lessons,

6
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:39,630
No pressure, just two people talking about life, learning English, daily habits, and

7
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:42,510
the small things that make our lives better.

8
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:43,510
Exactly.

9
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:48,470
Sometimes, the best way to learn English is simply by listening to real conversations.

10
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:54,030
When you hear natural speaking, your brain slowly starts to understand rhythm, vocabulary,

11
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:55,110
and expressions.

12
00:00:55,110 --> 00:00:56,230
That's true.

13
00:00:56,230 --> 00:01:01,910
Many learners focus only on grammar, but real communication is about understanding people

14
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:04,310
and expressing ideas naturally.

15
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:07,550
So today, we're just going to talk like friends.

16
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:09,830
Imagine you're sitting at a cafe with us,

17
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:12,270
listening to two people having a conversation.

18
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:13,510
That sounds nice.

19
00:01:13,510 --> 00:01:16,550
Actually, I love the atmosphere of cafes,

20
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:19,670
the quiet music, the sound of coffee machines,

21
00:01:19,670 --> 00:01:21,390
people chatting softly.

22
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:22,630
Same here.

23
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:26,190
A cafe is one of the best places to think or work.

24
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:28,350
Sometimes when I'm stuck on a problem,

25
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:30,590
I go to a cafe, order coffee,

26
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:33,070
And somehow my brain starts working again.

27
00:01:33,070 --> 00:01:34,270
That's interesting.

28
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:36,950
Maybe the environment helps your creativity.

29
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:38,110
I think so.

30
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:41,870
When you're always in the same place, like your room or your office,

31
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:43,910
your brain becomes too comfortable.

32
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:47,030
But when you change environments, new ideas appear.

33
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:50,910
That reminds me of something I read recently about creativity.

34
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:54,110
Many creative people change locations when they work.

35
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,790
Some write in cafes, some go to parks,

36
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:58,630
some travel to different cities.

37
00:01:58,630 --> 00:02:02,310
Travel is another great topic. Do you enjoy traveling?

38
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:07,110
I really do. Traveling gives you a completely different perspective on life.

39
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:11,430
When you visit new places, meet new people, and see different cultures,

40
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:15,030
you realize the world is much bigger than your daily routine.

41
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:19,350
That's so true. When people stay in the same place for too long,

42
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:22,470
they sometimes forget how diverse the world is.

43
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:27,830
Exactly. Even simple things like food can be completely different in another country.

44
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:31,270
Oh, yes. Food is one of the best parts of traveling.

45
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:35,190
Definitely. Every culture expresses itself through food.

46
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:40,630
Spices, cooking techniques, traditions, everything tells a story.

47
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:44,390
I remember the first time I tried street food in another country.

48
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:46,230
I was a little nervous at first.

49
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:47,430
Why nervous?

50
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:50,230
Well, because it looked different from what I was used to.

51
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:54,710
The cooking style, the ingredients, the smells, everything was new.

52
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:55,830
But did you like it?

53
00:02:55,830 --> 00:03:02,790
I loved it. It was one of the best meals I ever had. And it was very simple. Just a small dish from a street vendor.

54
00:03:02,790 --> 00:03:07,750
That's the beauty of food. Sometimes the simplest meals are the most memorable.

55
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:12,870
Speaking of memorable experiences, do you remember the first time you started learning English?

56
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:17,830
Oh yes, very clearly. It was actually quite confusing at the beginning.

57
00:03:17,830 --> 00:03:19,910
Really? What was difficult about it?

58
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:25,830
Everything felt new, the pronunciation, the grammar, the sentence structure.

59
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:28,310
It felt like learning how to think again.

60
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:30,950
That's a great way to describe language learning.

61
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:34,710
You're not just learning words, you're learning a new way of thinking.

62
00:03:34,710 --> 00:03:37,830
Exactly. And at first, I was afraid to speak.

63
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:40,230
That's very common for language learners.

64
00:03:40,230 --> 00:03:42,870
Yes, because you worry about making mistakes.

65
00:03:42,870 --> 00:03:45,190
You worry about sounding strange.

66
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:48,630
But the truth is, mistakes are the fastest way to learn.

67
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:51,990
Absolutely. Every mistake teaches you something.

68
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:57,630
I always tell learners that fluency doesn't come from perfection. It comes from practice.

69
00:03:57,630 --> 00:04:02,250
That's such an important idea. Many people late until their English is perfect before

70
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:03,630
they start speaking.

71
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:05,550
And that day never comes.

72
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:06,470
Exactly.

73
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:09,870
The real secret is speaking early and speaking often.

74
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:14,750
Yes, even if your sentences are simple, communication is still happening.

75
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:16,170
Let me ask you something.

76
00:04:16,170 --> 00:04:18,970
What do you think is the biggest challenge for English learners?

77
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:22,350
I think confidence is the biggest challenge.

78
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:23,350
Interesting.

79
00:04:23,350 --> 00:04:24,350
Not grammar.

80
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:29,310
Grammar is important, but confidence controls whether you actually use the language.

81
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:30,430
That makes sense.

82
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:34,870
If someone knows grammar but never speaks, their progress will be slow.

83
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:36,190
Exactly.

84
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:39,230
Language is a skill, like playing a musical instrument.

85
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:40,950
That's a great comparison.

86
00:04:40,950 --> 00:04:45,790
Find someone who reads about playing guitar every day but never touches a guitar.

87
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:48,030
They will never become a good guitarist.

88
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:49,030
Exactly.

89
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:51,150
The same thing happens with languages.

90
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:52,870
You must practice speaking.

91
00:04:52,870 --> 00:04:55,430
Yes, and also listening.

92
00:04:55,430 --> 00:04:57,230
Listening is very powerful.

93
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:01,630
When you listen to English regularly, your brain starts recognizing patterns.

94
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:04,230
That's why podcasts are so useful.

95
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:08,590
Yes, podcasts allow learners to hear natural conversations.

96
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:10,870
And the best part is that you can listen anywhere.

97
00:05:10,870 --> 00:05:13,250
in the car, while walking, while cooking?

98
00:05:13,250 --> 00:05:14,250
Exactly!

99
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:19,350
Actually, I know someone who improved their English dramatically just by listening to podcasts

100
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:20,350
every day.

101
00:05:20,350 --> 00:05:21,350
Really?

102
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:22,350
What was their routine?

103
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:26,190
They listened for about 30 minutes each morning during their commute.

104
00:05:26,190 --> 00:05:27,390
That's a great habit.

105
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:29,950
After a few months, they noticed something interesting.

106
00:05:29,950 --> 00:05:30,950
What happened?

107
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:32,910
They started thinking in English.

108
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:35,510
That's a huge milestone in language learning.

109
00:05:35,510 --> 00:05:40,670
Yes, when your brain starts forming thoughts directly in English instead of translating.

110
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:43,110
translation can slow down communication.

111
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:44,270
Exactly.

112
00:05:44,270 --> 00:05:48,830
When you're translating every sentence in your head, conversations become stressful.

113
00:05:48,830 --> 00:05:52,630
But when you think directly in English, everything becomes smoother.

114
00:05:52,630 --> 00:05:54,430
Let me ask you another question.

115
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:58,590
Do you think learning English is different today compared to 20 years ago?

116
00:05:58,590 --> 00:05:59,590
Definitely.

117
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:00,950
In what way?

118
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,790
Technology has completely changed language learning.

119
00:06:03,790 --> 00:06:04,790
That's true.

120
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:08,550
In the past, people depended on textbooks and classroom lessons.

121
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:11,870
And maybe CDs or cassette tapes for listening practice.

122
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:12,870
Exactly.

123
00:06:12,870 --> 00:06:19,390
But today we have videos, podcasts, language apps, online teachers, and communities.

124
00:06:19,390 --> 00:06:22,550
The internet made learning much more accessible.

125
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:24,990
Anyone with a phone can start learning today.

126
00:06:24,990 --> 00:06:27,350
But there is also a small problem.

127
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:28,350
What's that?

128
00:06:28,350 --> 00:06:29,350
Too many resources.

129
00:06:29,350 --> 00:06:31,070
That's actually true.

130
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:34,670
Some learners jump from one method to another every week.

131
00:06:34,670 --> 00:06:36,830
That can slow progress.

132
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:38,830
Reading is more important than variety.

133
00:06:38,830 --> 00:06:42,710
Yes, it's better to follow one routine regularly.

134
00:06:42,710 --> 00:06:45,150
For example, listening practice every day.

135
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:47,430
Speaking practice several times a week.

136
00:06:47,430 --> 00:06:50,070
And reading something interesting in English.

137
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:51,350
Reading is underrated.

138
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:52,630
I agree.

139
00:06:52,630 --> 00:06:55,230
Reading helps expand vocabulary naturally.

140
00:06:55,230 --> 00:07:00,150
When you read stories or articles, you see words used in real context.

141
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:02,150
Which makes them easier to remember.

142
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:03,150
Exactly.

143
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:06,070
Do you prefer reading physical books or digital books?

144
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:07,870
That's a difficult question.

145
00:07:07,870 --> 00:07:08,470
Why?

146
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:10,770
Because I like both for different reasons.

147
00:07:10,770 --> 00:07:11,670
Tell me more.

148
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:14,070
Physical books feel more relaxing.

149
00:07:14,070 --> 00:07:17,970
There's something special about holding a book and turning pages.

150
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:19,370
I feel the same way.

151
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:21,370
But digital books are convenient.

152
00:07:21,370 --> 00:07:24,470
You can carry hundreds of books on one device.

153
00:07:24,470 --> 00:07:26,670
That's true, especially when traveling.

154
00:07:26,670 --> 00:07:29,170
Yes, luggage becomes much lighter.

155
00:07:29,170 --> 00:07:32,770
Another advantage of digital reading is built-in dictionaries.

156
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:35,570
That's incredibly useful for language learners.

157
00:07:35,570 --> 00:07:38,610
If you see a new word, you can check the meaning instantly.

158
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:40,690
No need to open a separate dictionary.

159
00:07:40,690 --> 00:07:46,290
Exactly. But regardless of format, the most important thing is reading regularly.

160
00:07:46,290 --> 00:07:47,970
Consistency, again.

161
00:07:47,970 --> 00:07:52,050
Yes, small daily habits create big results over time.

162
00:07:52,050 --> 00:07:55,810
That's a powerful idea that applies to many areas of life.

163
00:07:55,810 --> 00:07:57,170
Not just language learning.

164
00:07:57,170 --> 00:07:58,050
Exactly.

165
00:07:58,050 --> 00:08:03,410
Health, career, relationships, everything improves with small consistent actions.

166
00:08:03,410 --> 00:08:05,490
Let's talk about habits for a moment.

167
00:08:05,490 --> 00:08:05,990
Sure.

168
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:07,750
Do you have a morning routine?

169
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:09,170
Yes, actually I do.

170
00:08:09,170 --> 00:08:11,030
I'm curious, what does it look like?

171
00:08:11,030 --> 00:08:12,450
I try to keep it simple.

172
00:08:12,450 --> 00:08:15,010
First, I wake up and drink a glass of water.

173
00:08:15,010 --> 00:08:16,290
That's a healthy start.

174
00:08:16,290 --> 00:08:18,170
Then I stretch for a few minutes.

175
00:08:18,170 --> 00:08:20,570
Stretching in the morning feels great.

176
00:08:20,570 --> 00:08:21,850
It wakes up the body.

177
00:08:21,850 --> 00:08:23,210
What happens after that?

178
00:08:23,210 --> 00:08:26,090
I usually read something for about 15 minutes.

179
00:08:26,090 --> 00:08:27,330
What kind of reading?

180
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:29,730
Sometimes a book, sometimes an article.

181
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:32,290
That sounds like a peaceful way to begin the day.

182
00:08:32,290 --> 00:08:36,290
It really is. Starting the day slowly helps me think clearly.

183
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:39,290
Many people start their day by checking their phones.

184
00:08:39,290 --> 00:08:42,290
Yes, and that can create stress immediately.

185
00:08:42,290 --> 00:08:45,290
Notifications, messages, emails.

186
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:48,290
Exactly. Your brain suddenly feels overwhelmed.

187
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:51,290
Maybe a calm start is better.

188
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:52,290
I believe so.

189
00:08:52,290 --> 00:08:55,290
After your reading time, what do you do next?

190
00:08:55,290 --> 00:08:56,290
Coffee.

191
00:08:56,290 --> 00:08:57,290
Of course.

192
00:08:57,290 --> 00:08:58,290
Coffee is essential.

193
00:08:58,290 --> 00:09:00,290
Many people would agree with you.

194
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:03,410
Something comforting about the smell of fresh coffee.

195
00:09:03,410 --> 00:09:05,410
Coffee culture is fascinating.

196
00:09:05,410 --> 00:09:06,850
It really is.

197
00:09:06,850 --> 00:09:09,770
Different countries have very different coffee traditions.

198
00:09:09,770 --> 00:09:12,090
Some prefer strong espresso.

199
00:09:12,090 --> 00:09:14,450
Others enjoy large cups of drip coffee.

200
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:17,650
And some cultures treat coffee as a social event.

201
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:19,970
Sitting together and talking for hours.

202
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:21,090
Exactly.

203
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:25,890
Speaking of conversations, do you think people talk less face to face today because of

204
00:09:25,890 --> 00:09:26,890
technology?

205
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:28,570
Matt's a deep question.

206
00:09:28,570 --> 00:09:29,570
I know.

207
00:09:29,570 --> 00:09:35,010
I think technology changed communication, but not necessarily reduced it.

208
00:09:35,010 --> 00:09:35,810
How so?

209
00:09:35,810 --> 00:09:39,570
People still communicate a lot, but the format has changed.

210
00:09:39,570 --> 00:09:42,370
You mean messages instead of conversations?

211
00:09:42,370 --> 00:09:46,450
Yes. Texting, voice notes, social media comments.

212
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:49,730
Those forms are quicker, but sometimes less personal.

213
00:09:49,730 --> 00:09:50,610
Exactly.

214
00:09:50,610 --> 00:09:54,770
A real conversation allows emotions, tone, and body language.

215
00:09:54,770 --> 00:09:56,930
Which makes communication richer.

216
00:09:56,930 --> 00:09:57,890
Exactly.

217
00:09:57,890 --> 00:10:00,370
That's why podcasts feel special.

218
00:10:00,370 --> 00:10:04,690
Yes, because listeners hear real voices and natural interaction.

219
00:10:04,690 --> 00:10:06,290
It feels more human.

220
00:10:06,290 --> 00:10:07,170
Exactly.

221
00:10:07,170 --> 00:10:10,690
I hope our listeners feel like they are part of this conversation.

222
00:10:10,690 --> 00:10:11,490
Me too.

223
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:14,690
Maybe they're listening while walking somewhere right now.

224
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:16,210
Or sitting on a train.

225
00:10:16,210 --> 00:10:17,810
Or studying at home.

226
00:10:17,810 --> 00:10:19,810
Wherever you are, thanks for listening.

227
00:10:19,810 --> 00:10:21,810
Yes, we really appreciate it.

228
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:26,930
And remember, every minute you spend listening to English is helping your brain improve.

229
00:10:26,930 --> 00:10:29,690
Language learning is like building a muscle.

230
00:10:29,690 --> 00:10:32,410
The more you train it, the stronger it becomes.

231
00:10:32,410 --> 00:10:33,450
Exactly.

232
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:36,010
And progress might feel slow at first.

233
00:10:36,010 --> 00:10:37,730
But if you stay consistent,

234
00:10:37,730 --> 00:10:40,410
one day you'll suddenly realize something.

235
00:10:40,410 --> 00:10:41,330
What will that be?

236
00:10:41,330 --> 00:10:43,130
You'll understand conversations

237
00:10:43,130 --> 00:10:45,050
that once felt impossible.

238
00:10:45,050 --> 00:10:47,530
That moment is incredibly rewarding.

239
00:10:47,530 --> 00:10:49,650
It makes all the effort worth it.

240
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:50,850
Absolutely.

241
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:52,690
Before we finish today's episode,

242
00:10:52,690 --> 00:10:55,090
I want to ask one final question.

243
00:10:55,090 --> 00:10:55,930
I'm listening.

244
00:10:55,930 --> 00:11:00,730
If someone listening today feels stuck in their English learning journey, what advice would

245
00:11:00,730 --> 00:11:01,730
you give them?

246
00:11:01,730 --> 00:11:03,070
That's a great question.

247
00:11:03,070 --> 00:11:04,070
Take your time.

248
00:11:04,070 --> 00:11:05,850
I would tell them three simple things.

249
00:11:05,850 --> 00:11:07,410
Okay, let's hear them.

250
00:11:07,410 --> 00:11:09,370
First, don't compare yourself.

251
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:12,590
Hi, everyone.

252
00:11:12,590 --> 00:11:15,350
Welcome back to our English learning podcast.

253
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:19,310
If you're here to improve your English and learn how to speak more naturally in

254
00:11:19,310 --> 00:11:22,430
everyday situations, you're in the right place.

255
00:11:22,430 --> 00:11:23,630
Hello, everyone.

256
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:25,710
We're really happy you're here today.

257
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:30,470
Whether you're listening while commuting, working, or relaxing at home, we hope this

258
00:11:30,470 --> 00:11:34,910
conversation helps you improve your English step by step.

259
00:11:34,910 --> 00:11:36,070
Absolutely.

260
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:39,790
And today, we have a very practical and interesting topic.

261
00:11:39,790 --> 00:11:43,750
We're going to talk about problems at work and how to solve them.

262
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:45,070
That's right.

263
00:11:45,070 --> 00:11:48,070
Everyone experiences problems at work at some point.

264
00:11:48,070 --> 00:11:54,310
It could be stress, communication issues, time pressure, or disagreements with coworkers.

265
00:11:54,310 --> 00:11:55,430
Exactly.

266
00:11:55,430 --> 00:12:00,590
No workplace is perfect, but the important thing is learning how to deal with these problems

267
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:03,050
in a professional and calm way.

268
